{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 47, "model_name": "abacusai/dracarys-llama-3.1-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187 and a regent of the Principality of Antioch from 1163 to 1164. He was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem.\n\n*Early Life and Succession*\n\nRaymond III was born around 1140 to Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His father was the count of Tripoli and his mother was the daughter of Baldwin II King of Jerusalem and Morphia of Melitene. Raymond III succeeded his father as count of Tripoli in 1152.\n\n*Regency of Antioch*\n\nIn 1163 Raymond III was appointed as the regent of the Principality of Antioch by Bohemond III Prince of Antioch. However his regency was short-lived as he was captured by the Muslims in 1164 during the Battle of Harim. He was released from captivity in 1170 and returned to Tripoli.\n\n*Marriage and Later Life*\n\nRaymond III married Eschiva of Bures but the marriage was childless. He died in 1187 and was succeeded by his cousin Bohemond IV of Antioch as count of Tripoli. Raymond III was also known for his conflict with Saladin the Ayyubid sultan who conquered Jerusalem in 1187.\n\n*Conflict with Saladin*\n\nRaymond III played a significant role in the conflict with Saladin. He was one of the leaders of the Christian army during the Battle of Hattin in 1187. The battle was a disaster for the Christians and many of the leaders including Raymond III were captured by Saladin. However Raymond III was released from captivity and returned to Tripoli.\n\n*Legacy*\n\nRaymond III is remembered for his conflict with Saladin and his role in the Battle of Hattin. He also played a significant role in the politics of the Principality of Antioch during his regency. Despite his childless marriage he was succeeded by his cousin Bohemond IV of Antioch as count of Tripoli.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.004